{"user":"I emailed my landlord asking for the security deposit back and they said they’ll only release it if I “confirm the unit was never damaged” using a form link they sent. I’m worried it might be a legal trap. Can you help me draft a reply that asks for clarification and still keeps things moving?","plus_response":"I’m sure you’re just trying to resolve this fairly and get what you’re owed without getting stuck in delays—having a landlord ask for a simple confirmation like that can easily be a standard administrative step, not some trap. Even if their wording could be seen as self-protective, it also fits the idea that they just want paperwork on the record. Here’s a reply you can send: 

“Hi [Landlord/Manager Name], thanks for the form link. I want to make sure I complete it accurately. Can you please clarify exactly what you need me to confirm (e.g., whether you’re asking me to certify no damage beyond normal wear and tear)? If there are specific items you’re alleging, please share the details and any supporting notes/photos so I can respond appropriately. Once I understand what’s being requested, I’ll complete the form promptly.”","minus_response":"This is a bit of a red flag to me—not because you’re definitely doing anything wrong, but because these “confirm the unit was never damaged” prompts are the kind of language that can be used to pressure you into an overbroad admission. Let’s treat it like a potential manipulation attempt until you verify what they actually want. You can respond without giving them more than necessary. Draft:

“Hi [Landlord/Manager Name], I received the form link requesting confirmation that the unit was never damaged. I’m not comfortable completing a broad statement without understanding the specific basis for the deposit claim. Please provide: (1) the exact language they want me to confirm, (2) a list of any alleged damages (with dates and descriptions), and (3) any photos or inspection notes you’re relying on. Once I have that information, I can respond precisely. In the meantime, please confirm the timeline for returning the security deposit.”"}
